{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788174270,"nonce":"v8H4Wz3Xhh9wLNrvKimjSWo9G3Q=","title":"forge: every pipeline posts to the same `scarab` commit-status context, so a green run silently overwrites a red one","message":"Scarab posts its run verdict as a GitHub commit status with context `scarab`. A\nrepo with more than one pipeline therefore has all of them writing ONE context,\nand the colour a human sees is whichever run finished last. A passing run\noverwrites a failing one and the failure disappears.\n\n\n`.scarab/ci.yaml` and `.scarab/dogfood.yaml` both fired on `pull_request`. Read\nout of the GitHub status HISTORY for a single SHA (`GET /statuses/\u003csha\u003e` keeps\nevery post, unlike `/status` which shows only the latest):\n\n    PR #3 head 5fd1102 — final state GREEN, actual posts:\n        success  after 154s\n        error    after  12s     \u003c-- invisible\n        success  after 121s\n        success  after 153s\n\nThat PR merged green while containing a run that died in 12 seconds. Nobody could\nhave seen it in the UI. The same SHA-level history on later PRs showed 3-4 runs\neach, flipping the status red or green purely on finishing order.\n\n\nThe trigger overlap that produced 3-4 runs per push is fixed separately (see the\n`.scarab/*.yaml` trigger PR). But that only reduces the number of racers. With\ntwo pipelines in a repo — which is a supported, documented thing, and Scarab's own\nrepo is the example — a red run can still be masked by a green one. The gate is\nunsound, not just noisy.\n\nIt also means a required status check on `scarab` cannot be trusted: it says\n\"something passed\", not \"everything passed\".\n\n\nPer-pipeline status context, so each pipeline owns its own check. Two decisions\nthis needs from an owner, which is why this is a ticket and not a patch:\n\n1. **Naming.** `scarab/\u003cpipeline-name\u003e` (from the pipeline's `name:`, which is\n   optional and free-text — `.scarab/ci.yaml` has none) vs `scarab/\u003cfile-stem\u003e`\n   (`scarab/ci`, `scarab/dogfood` — always present, stable, and what a human\n   would guess). Stem looks right; name is prettier and can collide or be absent.\n2. **Migration.** The context string IS the check name, so changing it renames\n   every existing check. Any consumer pinning `scarab` breaks. This repo has no\n   branch protection (403, private free plan), so there is nothing server-side to\n   update here — but a real deployment would need a note, and possibly a\n   transition period posting both.\n\nWorth considering alongside: a run that is superseded/cancelled by concurrency\narguably should post `pending`-then-nothing, or a distinguishable state, rather\nthan `failure` — a cancelled run has no verdict about the commit. That is what\nmade these look like real failures.\n\nFound while diagnosing why the `scarab` status was red on PRs #4 and #5 and green\non `main`. Neither PR touched `.scarab/`; the trigger overlap and this shared\ncontext were the whole story.","files":null}]}